
=======================================================================
Overview and Critique of current design:

   - The Pscf::SweepTmpl class takes the type of data structure
     that stores a "State" as a template parameter.

   - The Sweep class in each program-level namespace is derived
     from a specialization of Pscf::SweepTmpl with a specific 
     choice of data type for a State. This forces the choice of 
     a single type of State class for any Sweep in a specified 
     program-level namespace.

   - This usage makes it impossible to create Sweep classes for
     periodic systems that are designed for use with either a 
     symmetry-preserving iterator, for which states should be 
     stored in symmetry-adapted basis format, or non-symmetry
     preserving iterators, for which the state should be stored
     using values on a grid.

   - This pattern encourages coding of implementation details that
     depend on the choice of State class into the program-level
     Sweep class, since is the highest level class that "knows" 
     about the choice of a specific State class type. 
 
   - Ideally, the the program-level Sweep class should only define 
     an public interface and perhaps some protected functions for
     operations that are guaranteed to be the same for all types 
     of Sweep subclasses. The SweepTmpl class that provide parts of
     an implementation for a specific type of state should be a
     subclass of the program-level "Sweep" class, rather than the
     reverse.

   - The Pscf::SweepTmpl class now provides code for two logically 
     separate purposes:

     (1) Use of Lagrange polynomial exptrapolation to create an 
     extrapolated initial conditions for each state in a sweep.

     (2) Maintenance of a list of specialized ParameterType objects
     for parameters that are not part of the standard list. 

     There is no overlap in data structures or functions used for
     these two purposes, and it seems clear that they really should
     be implemented in separate classes that are used by composition
     rather than inheritance.

